 ## 3. Decisions and rationale
+1. Open findings get `{ todo }` unit tests, as in round 2, so `npm test` stays green. S-8 and S-9 were fixed by the lead
+   while the review ran, so their tests are plain passing regression tests. S-1's fix differs from the 2026-10-11 test's
+   assumption (glob), so I added a test that runs the fix, and kept the old one untouched (not my file).
+2. S-8 is Medium. A scheduled job with a write token silently reverting other commits on the deploy branch, then
+   deploying an untested tree, is an integrity problem with no attacker needed, and it is likely given this repository's
+   commit rate. S-9 is Low, because exploiting it needs a compromised or buggy refresh job.
+3. S-10 is Low, unlike round 2's S-3 (Medium). These parsers only see the big-tech sites' own responses and Google's
+   titles in the crawler and the build, and no server-mode custom board reaches them.
+4. S-2 and S-4 are reported as residuals of their 2026-10-11 IDs, not as new IDs, with new tests. S-2 drops to Low,
+   because nothing is paused today.
+5. S-11 is Low. Nothing secret was found in the published checkpoints (four `untracked.tgz` from history were listed:
+   data/vetting scripts, screenshots, a lane log). The finding is the missing filter on an unattended push to a public
+   default branch, plus its coupling to the branch Pages build.
+6. The S-11 test runs `checkpoint.sh` for real against a scratch origin, a clone and a lane worktree under the OS temp
+   dir, without `--push`, and lists the tarball. That proves the behaviour, not a text pattern.
+7. Not reported:
+   - `aria-allowed-role` (minor) on `article role=button` and `aside role=dialog`, for the same reason as round 2.
+   - Hash values still have no length cap; `b=` is a new array key like the others, and was not re-measured.
+   - The DOL downloader's missing host pin and size cap. It is manual only, reads from dol.gov, and is in no
+     workflow; it is noted under "Checked and fine" as hygiene.
+8. A-11 was measured with the Amazon list held back by a route gate, so the preview stays up as long as the probe needs.
+   That stands in for the ~5 s a phone on Fast 4G shows the preview. Its e2e test does the same.
+9. The crafted build's own crafted salaries ($900K-$1.1M) were removed by the vetting gate as outliers, so they never
+   reached the preview. The second and third builds therefore made the three real postings that lead the vetted preview
+   hostile (title, department, team, first location, salary text), keeping their real pay.
+10. The full e2e suite ran from this worktree, with `dist` a symlink to the lead's build and `data/{snapshots,beta/
+    snapshots}` symlinks to the lead's crawl (`wt-data2`). Nothing in the shared checkout or the lead's build is written
+    (`data/cache` is the worktree's own and gitignored).
 
